#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171681 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/id #uuid "59510c92-d9be-57c4-bc6d-497107401785" :question/math-academy-id "e-10051" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=100$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64,$ we have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} = 100 \\cdot 0.64 = 64 > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 100 \\cdot (1-0.64) = 36 > 5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{|}} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.64\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.64(1-0.64)}{100}} \\\\  & 0.64\\pm 0.079\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171682 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/id #uuid "221d0cad-0cb8-5cdc-816e-19408a796d05" :question/math-academy-id "e-10054" :question/problem "A survey of a random sample of $1\\,500$ U.S. households reveals that $780$ of them own at least one dog. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one dog.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p.$* \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one dog, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\, 500,$ and\n\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one dog can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,} = \\dfrac{780}{1\\, 500} = 0.52\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} =1\\,500 \\cdot 0.52 = 780 > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 1\\,500 \\cdot (1-0.52) = 720 > 5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{|}} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z > z_{0.025}) = 0.025,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that $z_{0.025}=1.96{:}$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.025}$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.960}$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.52\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.52(1-0.52)}{1\\,500}} \\\\  & 0.52\\pm 0.025\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533b2f03-04f0-5527-a86b-f93d974d8939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65\\pm 0.093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505270} {:answer/id #uuid "74b3b9e5-732e-55d4-bfcd-89de2017dcc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65\\pm 0.201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505340} {:answer/id #uuid "99f8fee4-71b0-5ee3-8216-6c083e12a604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505405} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff0b0f4-ca7f-5884-9640-7d13e7460657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65\\pm 0.052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505418} {:answer/id #uuid "a04b9b8d-7c34-521e-9936-d84df2c4f5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "533b2f03-04f0-5527-a86b-f93d974d8939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65\\pm 0.093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505270} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb8692a1-74f0-5ff4-a1e6-f0adf87799e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b09786c-003f-565a-9fd2-fe6264490f01" :question/math-academy-id "q-142715" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $65%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since $n=100$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=65%=0.65,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=100\\cdot 0.65=65>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=100\\cdot (1-0.65)=35>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\n![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.65\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.65(1-0.65)}{100}} \\\\  & 0.65\\pm 0.093\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51bb8808-acc1-5bbe-be4c-18bb2a65467d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505266} {:answer/id #uuid "64697aae-ec23-5ab7-a338-517c91ef2dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.24\\pm 0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505306} {:answer/id #uuid "a10aa168-0f6b-5c3e-b6d3-52b5f81ecea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.017" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505422} {:answer/id #uuid "bf990066-e8e2-556a-a296-fcaab89dd3be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.24\\pm 0.091" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505480} {:answer/id #uuid "d30ed7ea-b91a-59ba-890e-8f565053e4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d30ed7ea-b91a-59ba-890e-8f565053e4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505510} :answer-field/id #uuid "8362fec0-95c3-51e3-8cbd-6c9d9645d33c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42e7a19f-f4f3-5e6a-9979-741a2870cc54" :question/math-academy-id "q-142726" :question/problem "A survey of $150$ randomly selected students at a particular university showed that only $24$ select at least one math course. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the proportion of students from the entire university who select at least one math course. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of the students at a particular university,\n\n- some of the students select at least one math course, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=150,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who select at least one math course can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{24}{150}=0.16\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=150\\cdot 0.16=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=150\\cdot (1-0.16)=126>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.16\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.16(1-0.16)}{150}} \\\\  & 0.16\\pm 0.059\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ecc0a7-c18b-5d04-8b82-55c9c806c090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.52\\pm 0.380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505106} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb55c69-b1d6-5bba-b48a-dfa81c0a824e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23\\pm 0.208" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505212} {:answer/id #uuid "420d7c1a-501f-56be-927d-99c9e5d936d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.803" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505233} {:answer/id #uuid "9154af0c-0d34-5d22-8cc5-0fbe90b92aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505385} {:answer/id #uuid "c601ed4b-5156-562f-8160-e837ab35aea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.031" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9154af0c-0d34-5d22-8cc5-0fbe90b92aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505385} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ab939d-eb1c-5644-9dfb-10535344778d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55f340e1-5420-5652-bd4b-d4b5bdb68c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143281" :question/problem "The financial manager of a large company conducts a random sample of $120$ accounts receivable and finds that $42$ of them are more than $30$ days past due. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all of the company's accounts receivable that are more than $30$ days past due. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all accounts receivable from a certain company,\n\n- some of the accounts selected are more than $30$ days past due, and some are not,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=120,$ and\n\n- the proportion of accounts receivable in the sample that are more than $30$ days past due can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{42}{120}=0.35\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.35=42>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.35)=78>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.35\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.35(1-0.35)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.35\\pm 0.112\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d4a80f-7422-50eb-a2d2-982d8ab608b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\pm 0.051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505112} {:answer/id #uuid "241b9168-9165-5486-a2bb-6a5b1833da28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13\\pm 0.551" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505165} {:answer/id #uuid "74bffb6d-6a48-5bce-aa53-0c42cab5e2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\pm 0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505341} {:answer/id #uuid "9df41cfb-274a-5f20-b768-014759fce6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13\\pm 0.515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505414} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0ae98a-0528-502d-83ac-663a3b219c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\pm 0.505" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03d4a80f-7422-50eb-a2d2-982d8ab608b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\pm 0.051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505112} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9ab8b3f-5403-5ce1-916c-6c3e8a70acc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5b158e8c-e5d9-51ed-a647-72626d39b20a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143307" :question/problem "A survey shows that $66$ out of a random sample of $220$ fans of a popular MLB baseball team are against the way their current coach has run the team.\nFind a $90%$ confidence interval for the proportion of the team's fans who are against the way the coach has run the team.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of fans of a certain MLB baseball team,\n\n- some of the fans are against the way their current coach has run the team, and some are not,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=220,$ and\n\n- the proportion of fans from the sample who are against the way the coach has run the team can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{66}{220}=0.3\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=220\\cdot 0.3=66>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=220\\cdot (1-0.3)=154>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2e/2ed94fdbb0eb4cc1dda3720592488546a02e157598e77a70fbd02d916906b56f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{220}} \\\\  & 0.3\\pm 0.051\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1396af2f-0e9b-507e-82c5-413a18a2648c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.014" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505140} {:answer/id #uuid "968a4e50-5eb2-5a76-9933-3d7484528b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505393} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3b9c13-ba57-5851-a89d-22737e7176b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505408} {:answer/id #uuid "be9971c7-2bc3-51c0-88a9-04a0c458bf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4\\pm 0.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505474} {:answer/id #uuid "f776c8d5-3609-5b90-9293-e091193acac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b3b9c13-ba57-5851-a89d-22737e7176b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505408} :answer-field/id #uuid "1704a036-d71b-5ebc-aa12-189bf93207bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec6576c2-76eb-5af7-a883-a898d0a92018" :question/math-academy-id "q-143313" :question/problem "A survey of $480$ randomly selected U.S. adults reveals that $336$ of the respondents have read at least one book in the past year. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. adults who read at least one book in the past year.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of  all U.S. adults,\n\n- some of the respondents read at least one book in the past year, and some didn't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=480,$ and\n\n- the proportion of adults who read at least one book in the past year can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{336}{480}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=480\\cdot 0.7=336>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=480\\cdot (1-0.7)=144>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{480}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.041\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d110045-4b65-57fd-8bdd-f29c56b9354b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.62\\pm 0.053" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505127} {:answer/id #uuid "16c22bf4-1ccd-5590-a353-2f8aba595ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.63\\pm 0.043" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505146} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfd3b35-f9d2-5dda-a6ab-2418f9a188a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.86\\pm 0.043" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505289} {:answer/id #uuid "6739d8fe-a3b3-5967-81be-436c26b89433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68\\pm 0.085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505312} {:answer/id #uuid "bdadb00d-76dd-5c2f-a3d9-6b6947e16f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68\\pm 0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdadb00d-76dd-5c2f-a3d9-6b6947e16f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68\\pm 0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505473} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c49a0d1-81cf-5551-b8ab-2f04901b402c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "139a4b78-66bf-579e-be6b-e3d4e7bd7403" :question/math-academy-id "q-143319" :question/problem "A survey of $1\\,200$ randomly selected U.S. households reveals that $816$ of them own at least one pet. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one pet.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one pet, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\,200,$ and\n\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one pet can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{816}{1\\,200}=0.68\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=1\\,200\\cdot 0.68=816>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=1\\,200\\cdot (1-0.68)=384>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,68\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.68(1-0.68)}{1\\,200}} \\\\  & 0.68\\pm 0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "254f74b7-918f-5b54-ab34-7dfd44a5c967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505168} {:answer/id #uuid "410ef78d-c017-5576-9592-1c5aedadc3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8\\pm 0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505231} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbc7a76-06b8-5674-a514-4c81e882c29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505333} {:answer/id #uuid "a2295786-3596-52cf-9fef-a42aad614fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8\\pm 0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505427} {:answer/id #uuid "ef863121-834a-5b79-bbdd-ab9220c8ef49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fbc7a76-06b8-5674-a514-4c81e882c29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505333} :answer-field/id #uuid "94296483-dea2-5dbb-8a1c-46540a814eae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd266226-8e47-5093-b6f1-e853e4a5f194" :question/math-academy-id "q-143371" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=120$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.2,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.2=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.2)=96>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.2\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.2\\pm 0.060\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a888794-f66d-5afe-bc58-23ba40e1a714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85\\pm 0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505125} {:answer/id #uuid "1b076401-8233-5f64-8e82-e1f1f914afe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85\\pm 0.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505152} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2c9957-7528-5f67-967a-1b97828bc052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15\\pm 0.078" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505221} {:answer/id #uuid "7d96a42b-49e1-507d-80f3-c1f792ef9a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15\\pm 0.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505356} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a6dcfc-fbd2-5ec7-b123-a5534cf1d684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15\\pm 0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d96a42b-49e1-507d-80f3-c1f792ef9a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15\\pm 0.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505356} :answer-field/id #uuid "0699f92d-9412-559f-a1e2-75949a0088e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af2f2f44-7a26-5871-833f-ef0bfbcbad94" :question/math-academy-id "q-143374" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=140$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $15%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=140$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=15%=0.15,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=140\\cdot 0.15=21>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=140\\cdot (1-0.15)=119>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{140}} \\\\  & 0.15\\pm 0.050\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f4f3b82-a533-5794-8ca9-4f94400ca4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505330} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9841ca-0e3c-51de-9459-0e80399403b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505479} {:answer/id #uuid "f803f0e2-f22e-5648-9673-d0d814ff166b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.086" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505587} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ed2da9-ae01-5f78-8c2d-a19c608ee3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.091" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505590} {:answer/id #uuid "fe71e4df-2883-5aae-aa32-c5d249040050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf9841ca-0e3c-51de-9459-0e80399403b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505479} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c12850-6cfd-5d6c-804a-41b990dd6bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acfb542c-f97d-5ef1-8816-235f54148d5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-225614" :question/problem "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=90$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.6,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=90$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.6,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=90\\cdot 0.6=54>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=90\\cdot (1-0.6)=36>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.6\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{90}} \\\\  & 0.6\\pm 0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4686a3da-8b92-5b7e-88d8-f689c1ced722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.057" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505250} {:answer/id #uuid "c525d632-4b9e-57b5-a500-ffa007743cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505486} {:answer/id #uuid "d375fed6-79ea-53af-816c-a4fa67ee017e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505512} {:answer/id #uuid "f6539426-1de9-5b1b-9e35-db0b7f08102d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.067" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505583} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2f4a63-cf19-57eb-86ff-e3b5b84510a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.088" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa2f4a63-cf19-57eb-86ff-e3b5b84510a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.088" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505594} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a0c197-f408-509a-9c1f-a1eeb5f17467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6190115-0559-574a-ba01-3e43de706837" :question/math-academy-id "q-225615" :question/problem "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=120$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.4=48>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.4)=72>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.088\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
